<?php

/**
 * ============================================================================================
 *  DRAFT -- GATE F. NOT ACTIVE. RECONCILED AGAINST PRODUCTION 2026-10-24.
 * ============================================================================================
 *
 * This file lives in database/migrations/_gate_f_pending/, which Laravel does NOT discover
 * (migration lookup is a non-recursive glob and nothing registers this path), and it is named
 * `*.php.draft` so it cannot match `*_*.php` even if someone later registers the directory.
 * Read README.md here before promoting it. tests/Unit/Database/MigrationHygieneTest.php asserts
 * both properties.
 *
 * P1-MIG-03 / #40. `products.cost_price` is READ AND WRITTEN as money by live code and is
 * declared by NO migration:
 *
 *   app/Services/OrderFinancialService.php:214        (float) ($product->cost_price ?? 0)  -- the
 *                                                      per-unit cost the whole margin calculation
 *                                                      rests on
 *   app/Services/StoreCurrencyConversionService.php:111  converts it on a store currency change
 *   app/Http/Controllers/Api/DashboardController.php:2335, :2482   writes it from request input
 *                                                      (validated `nullable|numeric|min:0|lte:price`)
 *   app/Http/Controllers/Api/DashboardController.php:984, :2589   reads it back for the dashboard
 *
 * WHY THIS ONE IS SEQUENCED BEFORE `store_order_financials`, and it is the reason this draft
 * exists at all rather than being folded into that table's: OrderFinancialService computes margin
 * as revenue minus `$unitCost`. With no column, `$product->cost_price` is null, `?? 0` makes the
 * unit cost ZERO, and EVERY P&L ROW IS ALL-MARGIN. Creating the financials table first would
 * therefore begin recording confident, wrong profit figures. The column has to exist, and be
 * populated, before the subsystem it feeds is switched on.
 *
 * TYPE, MEASURED NOT GUESSED: the column ALREADY EXISTS in production as
 * `decimal(15,2) NOT NULL DEFAULT 0.00`, read from a restored copy on 2026-10-24. That is what is
 * declared below. The former standing decimal(18,4) decision does not apply to a column
 * production already owns.
 *
 * AND THE DEFAULT IS THE FINDING. `NOT NULL DEFAULT 0.00` gives the schema NO WAY TO SAY
 * "cost unknown": a merchant who has never entered a cost is stored identically to one whose cost
 * is genuinely zero. Measured consequence in the same dump -- 36 of 45 products read 0.00, and 89
 * of 92 `store_order_financials` rows therefore carry `product_cost = 0` and a `gross_profit`
 * equal to full revenue. Those rows are NOT evidence that this migration ran late: recomputing
 * every one against today's costs changes NOTHING (0 of 89 would become non-zero), so the cause
 * is unentered cost data, not ordering. Making the column nullable would separate the two cases,
 * but it is a live-column change and so is the owner's call, not this draft's.
 *
 * NULLABLE, DEFAULT NULL -- NOT `default(0)`, and this is the one decision in this file that
 * changes a number a human reads. A zero cost is indistinguishable from an unknown cost, and the
 * reporting path already treats null as zero at :214. Keeping existing rows NULL means "we never
 * recorded a cost for this product", which is the truth for every row that predates the column;
 * backfilling 0 would assert a cost of zero and make every historical product look infinitely
 * profitable. The reporting change that distinguishes the two is NOT in this draft -- it belongs
 * with store_order_financials -- and is named here so it is not lost.
 *
 * RECONCILED 2026-10-24 against `SHOW CREATE TABLE products` from a restored copy of the
 * production dump. The column ALREADY EXISTS there, so the hasColumn() guard below makes up() a
 * no-op in production and the production type stands. That is the intended outcome, and it means
 * promoting this draft changes nothing in production -- it only brings a fresh environment into
 * line. The dump
 * wins.
 */

use Illuminate\Database\Migrations\Migration;
use Illuminate\Database\Schema\Blueprint;
use Illuminate\Support\Facades\Schema;

return new class extends Migration
{
    public function up(): void
    {
        if (! Schema::hasTable('products') || Schema::hasColumn('products', 'cost_price')) {
            return;
        }

        Schema::table('products', function (Blueprint $table) {
            $table->decimal('cost_price', 15, 2)->default(0)->after('price');   // NOT NULL DEFAULT 0.00 in production
        });
    }

    /**
     * DELIBERATELY A NO-OP, for the same reason 0001-0006 give for the six tables.
     *
     * MEASURED 2026-10-24 against a restored copy of the production dump: `products.cost_price`
     * ALREADY EXISTS there (`decimal(15,2) NOT NULL DEFAULT 0.00`) and holds 45 rows of merchant
     * cost data, 9 of them non-zero, summing to 1170.65. This migration's `up()` therefore
     * no-ops in production -- it did not create the column.
     *
     * The earlier version of this method dropped the column whenever it was PRESENT. Run against
     * the restored copy, `migrate:rollback` executed it in 30.28ms and DESTROYED all 45 values:
     * an up() that creates nothing paired with a down() that drops regardless. The asymmetry was
     * the defect, and it is why this is now a no-op rather than a guarded drop.
     *
     * A fresh environment is left with a spare zero-default column after a rollback. That is a
     * cosmetic wart; the alternative was irrecoverable loss of production money data. Drop it by
     * hand if you must.
     */
    public function down(): void
    {
    }
};
